<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:id="@+id/coordinatorLayout"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:fitsSystemWindows="true">

    <!-- ⚠⚠ COLLAPSING HEADER RESTORED 2026-09-07, REVERTING THE 2026-09-05 MIGRATION (5e4b9413). ⚠⚠
         This file is the pre-migration structure checked out verbatim from 5e4b9413^. The record below is
         carried forward BY HAND because it has no other copy — what is still true is kept, what the restore
         makes obsolete is MARKED OBSOLETE rather than deleted, so that nothing here reads as current
         guidance when it is not.

         WHAT WAS TRADED, KNOWINGLY: under a CollapsingToolbarLayout the fast-scroll rail can only span the
         RecyclerView, and HeaderScrollingViewBehavior sizes that view to `viewport - collapsedHeight` and
         positions it BELOW the header. So the rail begins below the header and THE THUMB IS NOT GRABBABLE
         until the header has scrolled away. That is the accepted cost of this restore, not a defect to
         re-fix. Four models were built hunting a "displaced" thumb before it was understood as a rest
         position: the capture read offset=0 padTop=0 thumbFrac=0.000 — the thumb had always been at the
         top of a track that started too low. Drawing the rail higher does not work either: a
         RecyclerView.OnItemTouchListener only sees events delivered to the RecyclerView.

         ⚠️ OBSOLETE AS OF THIS RESTORE, KEPT SO IT IS NOT RE-DERIVED: the migration-era claim that
         "AppBarLayout.getTotalScrollRange() breaks its loop at i=0 and returns Math.max(0, 0) = 0 (decoded
         from the 1.14.0 AAR), so PixelFastScrollViewHelper's composite terms are both 0 and the arithmetic
         reduces to the flat-screen path exactly". That was TRUE ONLY WHILE NO CHILD CARRIED A `scroll`
         FLAG. The CollapsingToolbarLayout below does, so totalScrollRange is non-zero again and BOTH
         composite terms (appBarRange, appBarConsumed) go live with no code change. The decode itself
         remains correct and is worth keeping: it is why the terms read 0 on every non-collapsing screen.
         See the note at FastScrollerHelper.applyTo.

         ⚠️ OBSOLETE: "the cover moved into MediaHeaderAdapter (item 0); the TITLE did not follow it there
         — it is set on the toolbar instead". Both halves are reversed now. The CTL owns the cover
         (coverContainer/cover below, loaded by MediaFragment), and the toolbar title is the COLLAPSED title
         with the CTL's expandedTitleTextAppearance providing the expanded one. ONE OWNER EACH — that is the
         hybrid this restore had to avoid: a CTL with the header content still drawing a title renders the
         name twice, and a CTL with the toolbar title still deleted leaves the collapsed bar nameless.

         RESTORED WITH IT, DELIBERATELY: MediaFragment's `if (isTV) binding.appBarLayout.setExpanded(false,
         false)`. That decision was dead while the header could not collapse; it is live again.

         ── paddingBottom="49dp" ON THE ConstraintLayout BELOW — READ BEFORE TOUCHING ──
         It is back, and it is the ONLY owner of the cover-to-buttons gap again. History, because it has
         been got wrong twice: it is residue of the CTL's circular measurement dependency (AppBarLayout and
         CTL both wrap_content, CTL height scaling ~1.5x paddingBottom rather than 1:1, cover size and
         expanded-title space contending through one knob), settled empirically at 43dp on 2026-06-15 after
         three uiautomator dumps and later carried at 49dp. The migration deleted it on the reasoning "the
         CTL is gone, none of those inputs exist" — correct as far as it went, but IT MISSED THAT THE
         PADDING WAS DOING A SECOND JOB: it sat below the cover, so its visual effect was the
         cover-to-buttons gap, which is a design element that outlived the CTL. Deleting it flattened that
         gap to 0 and was reported from device as a regression on artist, album and playlist. It was then
         rebuilt in item_media_header.xml as 57dp on the cover wrapper, later split to 4dp + 22dp.
         ⚠️ THAT REBUILD IS NOW DELETED, and it must stay deleted: with 49dp back here, keeping the wrapper
         padding as well would DOUBLE-COUNT the gap. One owner.

         ⚠️ DO NOT RE-PROPOSE THE OVERLAP. TRIED AND REVERTED, 2026-09-06 to 2026-09-07 (e787edd6 ->
         c9649426). For one build the FragmentContainerView carried a custom OverlapScrollingViewBehavior
         instead of @string/appbar_scrolling_view_behavior so the list spanned the full viewport and the art
         scrolled under a transparent, scrimmed toolbar — an attempt to get the rail to span the screen
         WITHOUT a collapsing header. It produced FIVE defects at once, all reported from device on album
         and artist:
           1. The cover was CLIPPED AT THE TOP — its upper edge ran under the status bar.
           2. appbarOutline drew a HORIZONTAL LINE ACROSS THE MIDDLE OF THE ARTWORK (anchored to the
              AppBarLayout's bottom, which fell inside the cover instead of above it).
           3. The scrim DID NOT VISIBLY DARKEN ANYTHING — back arrow and overflow floated over bright art.
           4. Mid-scroll the title rendered TWICE AND OVERLAPPING, header content and toolbar: the
              scroll-driven fade never worked and the toolbar title was solid throughout.
           5. The thumb RESTED BEHIND THE TOOLBAR and could not be grabbed — the exact problem the overlap
              existed to solve, reintroduced one layer up.
         It needs the outline anchor, the scrim, the scroller padding, the cover inset and the title fade
         ALL SOLVED TOGETHER; none of them was. And symptom 5 means it never delivered the benefit it cost
         five defects to attempt. Anyone reviving it is starting a five-part piece of work, not flipping an
         attribute. @drawable/bg_toolbar_scrim was deleted with it.

         THE 2026-06-13 TITLE WORK IS BACK, BUT NOT ALL IN THIS FILE: expandedTitleMarginEnd and maxLines
         are inline attributes below; titleCollapseMode=fade comes via style="@style/EchoCollapsingBar"
         (styles.xml:41-43), not from a line here — do not add it inline, and do not conclude it is missing.
         `expandedTitleEllipsize` is genuinely absent and must stay absent: it does not exist in Material
         1.14.0 (confirmed by inspecting the AAR's attrs.xml directly, not from documentation).
         No contentScrim is set here and none ever was: @style/EchoCollapsingBar never had one, and the only
         contentScrim line in the project is a commented-out item in @style/TopCollapsingBar. The multi-line
         title overlapping the cover during expansion is standard Material 3 CTL behaviour with no
         contentScrim — recorded as such at the time, and unchanged by this restore. -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout
        android:id="@+id/appBarLayout"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:fitsSystemWindows="true">

        <!-- ⚠️ expandedTitleMarginBottom BELOW DELIBERATELY SUPERSEDES THE STYLE. @style/TopCollapsingBar
             (styles.xml:34, inherited through @style/EchoCollapsingBar) sets it to 20dp; the inline 14dp
             here is the tuned value and wins. READING styles.xml ALONE WOULD SAY THE 20 → 14 TUNING WAS
             LOST — it was not, it lives in this file. The same pass that dropped it also raised
             @dimen/media_header_cover_size 160 → 220 → 250dp, and all of these contend through the CTL's
             circular measurement (see the paddingBottom note above), so changing one without the others
             re‑opens a tuning that took three uiautomator dumps to settle.
             expandedTitleMarginStart/End (24dp) and titleCollapseMode=fade come from the style and are NOT
             overridden here; do not add them inline. -->
        <com.google.android.material.appbar.CollapsingToolbarLayout
            android:id="@+id/collapsingToolbar"
            style="@style/EchoCollapsingBar"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            app:expandedTitleMarginBottom="14dp"
            app:expandedTitleMarginEnd="24dp"
            app:expandedTitleTextAppearance="@style/MediaHeaderTitle"
            app:maxLines="2">

            <androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:paddingTop="16dp"
                android:paddingBottom="49dp">

                <androidx.cardview.widget.CardView
                    android:id="@+id/coverContainer"
                    style="@style/ItemCover"
                    tools:ignore="MissingConstraints">

                    <ImageView
                        android:id="@+id/cover"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="match_parent"
                        android:importantForAccessibility="no"
                        tools:ignore="ImageContrastCheck" />

                </androidx.cardview.widget.CardView>

            </androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>


            <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
                android:id="@+id/toolBar"
                style="@style/Toolbar"
                android:background="@android:color/transparent"
                app:menu="@menu/more_menu"
                tools:title="@tools:sample/full_names" />

        </com.google.android.material.appbar.CollapsingToolbarLayout>

    </com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout>

    <View
        android:id="@+id/appbarOutline"
        style="@style/AppBarOutline"
        app:layout_anchor="@id/appBarLayout"
        app:layout_anchorGravity="bottom" />

    <androidx.fragment.app.FragmentContainerView
        android:id="@+id/mediaFragmentContainer"
        android:name="dev.brahmkshatriya.echo.ui.media.MediaDetailsFragment"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        app:layout_behavior="@string/appbar_scrolling_view_behavior"
        tools:layout="@layout/fragment_media_details" />

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>